// ============================================================================
// Project 25 (Meridian Pay) -- Session 3, Phase 2
// 08_dedupe_fix.kql                       [REV 3 -- 2026-08-18]
//
// Fixes the two MISMATCH rows from 07_reconcile_after_load.kql:
//     auth_curated       expected 5,365,392   loaded 5,383,455   delta +18,063
//     telemetry_curated  expected 4,319,781   loaded 4,336,798   delta +17,017
//
// RUN ONE STATEMENT AT A TIME. Blank-line separated.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE DEFECT: AN UPDATE POLICY CANNOT DEDUPLICATE GLOBALLY
//
// Kusto evaluates an update policy PER INGESTED BATCH (per extent), never
// across the whole table. So
//     raw_auth_events | summarize arg_max(ingest_time, *) by auth_id
// removes only duplicates whose original lands in the SAME batch.
//
// The generator makes the split near-certain: dq_faults.duplicate_rows() does
//     out = pd.concat([df, dupes], ignore_index=True)
// -- appending all duplicates to the END of the frame, unshuffled. At 250,000-
// row ingest batches, all 21,477 auth duplicates land in the final batch while
// their originals sit across the preceding 5.36m rows. Only 3,414 of 21,477
// (auth) and 270 of 17,287 (telemetry) were caught.
//
// This is what contract Sec5's "independent downstream structural check" was
// written for: every command succeeded, the policy ran clean, the table
// filled, and the dedupe silently did almost nothing. Only a check that knew
// the expected DISTINCT-KEY count could tell "cleaned" from "copied".
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// REV HISTORY OF THIS FIX -- three platform limits hit in sequence, kept
// visible because each one is a real constraint a reader will also hit:
//
//   Rev 1  .set-or-replace auth_curated <| auth_curated | summarize ...
//          -> ClientAdminCommandFromQueryBadRequestException.
//          Kusto refuses .set-or-replace when the source query reads the
//          DESTINATION table. (.set-or-replace dim_estate <| fn_build_dim_estate()
//          worked precisely because that function reads stg_dim_*, not
//          dim_estate.) Wrapping the query in a function does NOT help -- Kusto
//          resolves the function body.
//          FIX: stage through a second table.
//
//   Rev 2  Staged version threw the throttling error
//          "CommandType: 'TableSetOrReplace', Capacity: 1,
//           Origin: 'CapacityPolicy/Ingestion'"
//          while the three materialized views were building their first real
//          snapshots. Transient; retry after they report Completed.
//          (Recorded as gate C3 evidence -- this capacity is shared with the
//          Order Management workspace, and a routine .set-or-replace on a
//          5.4m-row table is enough to contend for it.)
//
//   Rev 3  Retry then threw LowMemoryCondition:
//          KustoServicePartialQueryFailureLowMemoryConditionException.
//          arg_max(ingest_time, *) must hold all 19 columns for every group
//          across 5.38m rows -- more than this capacity will allocate.
//          FIX: take_any(*) instead. See below.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// WHY take_any(*) IS CORRECT HERE, NOT JUST CHEAPER.
// The injected duplicates are EXACT copies -- dq_faults.duplicate_rows() does
// `dupes = df.iloc[dup_idx].copy()`, identical natural key AND every other
// field, per GENERATOR_SPEC.md Sec6 rule 1. When every row in a group is
// byte-identical, "keep the one with the latest ingest_time" and "keep any
// one" return the same row. arg_max was doing expensive comparison work to
// choose between indistinguishable candidates. take_any is Kusto's documented
// dedupe idiom and is bounded in memory because it stops at the first row per
// group.
//
// STREAMING NUANCE, worth stating rather than leaving implicit: if live replay
// (Session 4) ever re-delivers an event with a DIFFERENT ingest_time -- a true
// at-least-once redelivery rather than an exact copy -- then take_any and
// arg_max diverge, and arg_max(ingest_time, *) is the semantically correct
// choice. For this backfill they are equivalent. Revisit when the dedup
// materialized view is built (see the open item at the end of this file).
// ============================================================================

// ---- STEP 1: cheap, memory-bounded dedupe function ------------------------

.create-or-alter function fn_dedupe_auth() { auth_curated | summarize take_any(*) by auth_id }

// ---- STEP 2: build the staging table --------------------------------------
// If this throws the CapacityPolicy/Ingestion throttle again, wait 2-3 minutes
// and re-run the identical statement -- it is transient, not a defect.

.set-or-replace auth_curated_dedup <| fn_dedupe_auth()

// ---- STEP 3: verify BEFORE destroying anything ----------------------------
// Must be exactly 5,365,392. Stop here if it is not.

auth_curated_dedup | count

// ---- STEP 4: swap in ------------------------------------------------------

.set-or-replace auth_curated <| auth_curated_dedup

// ---- STEP 5: reclaim ------------------------------------------------------

.drop table auth_curated_dedup

// ---- STEP 6-10: same sequence for telemetry -------------------------------

.create-or-alter function fn_dedupe_telemetry() { telemetry_curated | summarize take_any(*) by telemetry_id }

.set-or-replace telemetry_curated_dedup <| fn_dedupe_telemetry()

telemetry_curated_dedup | count

.set-or-replace telemetry_curated <| telemetry_curated_dedup

.drop table telemetry_curated_dedup

// ---- STEP 11: the independent structural check. Both must return 0. -------
// Not a count comparison -- a structural assertion that no natural key repeats.
// This is what 05_dq_rules_run.kql persists as rule 1's
// independent_check_passed, and it would have caught the original defect with
// no expected-count table at all.

auth_curated | summarize c = count() by auth_id | where c > 1 | count

telemetry_curated | summarize c = count() by telemetry_id | where c > 1 | count

// ---- STEP 12: confirm the update policies survived the extent swap --------

.show table auth_curated policy update

.show table telemetry_curated policy update

// ---- STEP 13: re-run 07_reconcile_after_load.kql -- expect 16/16 OK -------

// ============================================================================
// IF take_any(*) STILL HITS LowMemoryCondition
//
// Dedupe partitions cleanly by its own key -- every row sharing an auth_id
// hashes to the same bucket -- so the work can be split into 4 memory-bounded
// passes without changing the result. Replace STEP 2 with:
//
//   .set-or-replace auth_curated_dedup <| auth_curated | where hash(auth_id, 4) == 0 | summarize take_any(*) by auth_id
//   .append          auth_curated_dedup <| auth_curated | where hash(auth_id, 4) == 1 | summarize take_any(*) by auth_id
//   .append          auth_curated_dedup <| auth_curated | where hash(auth_id, 4) == 2 | summarize take_any(*) by auth_id
//   .append          auth_curated_dedup <| auth_curated | where hash(auth_id, 4) == 3 | summarize take_any(*) by auth_id
//
// The first is .set-or-replace (creates/clears), the rest .append. The count
// check in STEP 3 is unchanged and still exact -- which is the point of
// checking a number rather than checking that nothing errored.
//
// ============================================================================
// STILL OPEN -- THIS FILE FIXES THE BACKFILL, NOT THE STREAM
//
// Once live replay starts (Session 4), duplicates arriving in later batches
// will again survive the update policy. The platform's answer is a
// MATERIALIZED VIEW, which aggregates across all extents:
//     .create-or-alter materialized-view mv_auth_dedup on table auth_curated
//     { auth_curated | summarize arg_max(ingest_time, *) by auth_id }
//
// NOT done this session, deliberately -- it is a contract change, not a bug fix:
//   * OUTCOME_CONTRACT.md Sec3 commits to exactly 3 materialized views; two
//     dedup views make it 5. A committed number gets changed on purpose.
//   * Kusto cannot build a materialized view over another materialized view,
//     so the three aggregate MVs would keep reading un-deduped auth_curated --
//     ~0.4% inflation. Immaterial to reflex 1's thresholds (which carry
//     deliberate margin), but it is an inconsistency, and accepting it should
//     be an explicit decision rather than a later discovery.
//   * The streaming case is also where take_any vs arg_max stops being
//     equivalent (see the note above).
//
// ENGINE LESSON for PBIP_GENERATION_GUIDE.md at session close:
//   "An update policy is a per-batch transform. Any operation whose
//    correctness depends on seeing the whole table -- dedupe, global ranking,
//    cross-batch sequencing -- cannot live in one, and fails SILENTLY rather
//    than erroring. Use a materialized view. And prefer take_any(*) over
//    arg_max(key, *) for dedupe of exact duplicates: same answer, bounded
//    memory."
// ============================================================================
